---
const codeSample = [
  { tone: 'comment', text: '// features/steps/login.steps.ts' },
  { tone: 'plain', text: "When('the user signs in with a valid password', () => { /* ... */ });" },
  { tone: 'plain', text: '' },
  { tone: 'comment', text: '// features/steps/session.steps.ts  — added months later' },
  { tone: 'plain', text: "When('the user signs in with a valid password', () => { /* ... */ });" },
];

const toneColor: Record<string, string> = {
  comment: 'text-[#7d7396]',
  plain: 'text-[#ded8ef]',
};
---

<section class="border-t border-line bg-panel/40">
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-4 py-20 sm:px-6">
    <p class="text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-widest text-accent">The problem</p>
    <h2 class="mt-3 max-w-2xl text-3xl font-extrabold tracking-tight sm:text-4xl">
      Step libraries grow quietly — and start contradicting each other.
    </h2>
    <p class="mt-4 max-w-2xl leading-relaxed text-muted">
      In a growing Cucumber suite, new step definitions are added by many people over many months.
      Nobody notices the exact duplicate, the near-copy that only differs in a literal, or the
      definition no feature has used for a year — until steps become ambiguous and refactoring feels
      risky.
    </p>

    <div class="mt-12 grid gap-10 lg:grid-cols-2">
      <ul class="space-y-6">
        {[
          {
            title: 'Duplicates and near-duplicates',
            body: 'Two definitions match the same text — exactly, or after normalization. Whichever runs first wins.',
          },
          {
            title: 'Ambiguous feature steps',
            body: 'A concrete step in a .feature file matches multiple definitions, and the suite behaves unpredictably.',
          },
          {
            title: 'Copy-paste handlers',
            body: 'Handlers that differ only in literal values (`5 items` vs `10 items`) instead of using a step parameter.',
          },
          {
            title: 'Dead definitions',
            body: 'Steps no feature references anymore, still imported, still maintained, still misleading readers.',
          },
          {
            title: 'Rising maintenance cost',
            body: 'Every duplicated or unused step makes the suite harder to evolve and slower to reason about.',
          },
        ].map((item) => (
          <li class="flex gap-4">
            <span
              class="mt-1 inline-block h-2 w-2 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-accent"
              aria-hidden="true"
            ></span>
            <div>
              <p class="font-semibold">{item.title}</p>
              <p class="mt-1 leading-relaxed text-muted">{item.body}</p>
            </div>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>

      <div class="space-y-4">
        <div class="overflow-hidden rounded-xl border border-line bg-[var(--cd-terminal-bg)]">
          <div class="border-b border-[var(--cd-terminal-line)] bg-[var(--cd-terminal-panel)] px-4 py-2 font-mono text-xs text-[#7d7396]">
            two files, one matcher — CukeDedup flags it
          </div>
          <pre class="overflow-x-auto p-4 font-mono text-[12px] leading-relaxed"><code>{codeSample.map((line) => (
            <span class={`block whitespace-pre ${toneColor[line.tone]}`}>{line.text || ' '}</span>
          ))}</code></pre>
        </div>
        <div class="rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel p-5">
          <p class="text-sm font-semibold">The solution: analysis, not guesswork</p>
          <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">
            CukeDedup compares every discovered definition — matcher text, normalized forms, and
            handler structure — and reports eight classes of duplication, ambiguity, reuse, and
            disuse with suggested actions. It is an analysis tool: it tells you exactly what
            collides and why, and incomplete analysis is reported instead of treated as clean.
          </p>
          <a
            href="/docs/rules/"
            class="mt-3 inline-flex items-center gap-1 text-sm font-medium text-accent hover:underline"
          >
            See all eight rules →
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
